{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "p-subclass-elements-in-spell#list-1",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "Subclass Elements In Spell",
      "excerpt": "A plain view: a \"solitaire\" is a view . A view on a built-in base, the closest m",
      "text": "where is the code for this?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:11.356Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Two sided view\" is a built-in template",
      "offset": 128,
      "edited": "2026-11-19T23:30:16.156Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:45.492Z",
          "html": "<p>It's written in Spell itself, then compiled once into the core:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The template: <code title=\"parser/src/core/templates/TwoSidedView.spell\">TwoSidedView.spell</code>, beside its Spanish twin, <code title=\"parser/src/core/templates/TwoSidedView.es.spell\">TwoSidedView.es.spell</code>. It starts <code>a \"two sided view\" is a view</code>, with its slots front and back, its direction, <code>to turn it over</code>, and its drawing and CSS.</li>\n<li>The list of built-in templates (two sided view, site, page layout): <code title=\"parser/src/spell/builtInTemplates.ts\">builtInTemplates.ts</code>.</li>\n<li><code>yarn core:gen:templates</code> compiles each one into the core class <code title=\"parser/src/core/classes/TwoSidedView.ts\">TwoSidedView.ts</code>, and its declarations into <code title=\"parser/src/spell/builtInTemplates.generated.ts\">builtInTemplates.generated.ts</code>.</li>\n<li>So <code>a card is a two sided view</code> compiles to just the card's own members, and extends the core's <code>TwoSidedView</code>. P11 would do the same thing, with a Spell UI element as the base.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-19T23:30:32.039Z"
      }
    },
    "c6": {
      "anchor": "p-calling-typescript-helpers#list-7",
      "kind": "list",
      "label": "Calling TypeScript Helpers",
      "excerpt": "focus the first field of the form would hand focusFirstField(form) the form thin",
      "text": "this would traverse the DOM heirarchy to find the first spell `thing` of the appropriate type?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T23:39:44.207Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "core.elementOf",
      "offset": 370,
      "edited": "2026-11-19T23:40:13.891Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:49:54.937Z",
          "html": "<p><b>No, the other way round.</b> <code>core.elementOf(thing)</code> would start from a thing the program has, and find the element drawing it.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Today: element to thing (<code>SpellElement.thingOf()</code>), thing to id (<code>core.elementIdFor()</code>), and the runtime can already find a drawn element by id, inside views' shadow roots too.</li>\n  <li>So <code>elementOf()</code> would be: the thing's id, then that search. A thing drawn twice has two elements; the first would do.</li>\n  <li>No \"walk up to the nearest thing of a type\" exists, and P10 doesn't need one: Spell hands helpers things, never elements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The full answer, with the code, is in P10's text.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-19T23:44:57.495Z"
      }
    },
    "c7": {
      "anchor": "p-calling-typescript-helpers#code-2",
      "kind": "code",
      "label": "Calling TypeScript Helpers",
      "excerpt": "/** * Put `text` on the clipboard, as if the person had copied it. * - spell: co",
      "text": "Feels like embedding the spell in a comment is an anti-pattern.  \n\nI'm imagining that e.g. we'd have a library \n- code is in `clipboard.ts`\n- we'd have a `clipboard.spell` file that references that (in the package file is fine).  That would specify the languge/args names/etc\n- `clipboard.spell` would have some way of explicitlly calling the JS line (we'd need new spell for this)\n- `clipboard.spell` could be translated to `clipboard.es.spell` just like any other spell file\n\nNote that this feels like a lot of rope to hand people in terms of ambiguity, but it would be very cool.\n\nOne use for this, making language for the spell ui- components which have actions we'd want to call in thspell",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-19T23:40:59.903Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "edited": "2026-11-19T23:44:26.434Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-19T23:49:55.843Z",
          "html": "<p><b>Agreed.</b> Your library design is now Q9's option A, recommended; the docstring line drops to option E.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The library: <code>clipboard.ts</code> for the code, <code>Clipboard.spell</code> saying <code>to copy (text as text) to the clipboard</code>, and <code>Clipboard.es.spell</code> in Spanish.</li>\n  <li>Programs import the library exactly as today and only see the words.</li>\n  <li>Nothing in Spell can call JavaScript today. The one new thing is a statement in the <code>to</code> body, proposed as <code>run the code copyText with text</code>; its words wait for your OK.</li>\n  <li>Spell UI's actions fit the same shape, but they're methods on elements: a later question, leaning on C6's <code>core.elementOf()</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The worked example and the wording options are in P10's text.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-19T23:44:58.568Z"
      }
    },
    "c8": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-2",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "A page shows an uploader that takes .json files, picked or dropped, and lists th",
      "text": "drop-zone=\"#id` to drop on another element in the page, e.g. the enclosing <ui-section>",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:05:17.806Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "drop-zone=\"page\"",
      "offset": 493,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:05:42.349Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:02.439Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded in. <code>drop-zone</code> now takes a selector as well as <code>page</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>drop-zone=\"#editor\"</code>:  that element</li>\n<li>it looks first among the elements around the uploader, then the page, so <code>drop-zone=\"ui-section\"</code> is the section it sits in</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:00.687Z"
      }
    },
    "c9": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-2",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "A page shows an uploader that takes .json files, picked or dropped, and lists th",
      "text": "how would this work?  <spell-page> `when file dropped` handler?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:06:09.275Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "page can catch the upload",
      "offset": 610,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:06:22.168Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:03.862Z",
          "html": "<p>No <code>&lt;spell-page&gt;</code> handler is needed:  Spell already has the chain. Two ways, both in the plan (step 5):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>On the tag, which works today:  <code>&lt;ui-uploader on-ui-file-dropped={read the netlist from the file}&gt;</code> in a view's drawing, as a calculator's buttons write <code>onClick={...}</code>.</li>\n<li>In the outline:  the core sends the uploader's events into Spell's event chain (<code>send()</code>), starting at the view that drew it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It goes up until a handler takes it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the view's <code>- when a file is dropped on it:</code></li>\n<li>the lists holding the view</li>\n<li>the running app</li>\n<li>last, a program file's own <code>on file dropped with a file:</code>, your \"page\" handler</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A handler that runs takes it;  <code>pass event</code> hands it on up. What's built:  the drop handler, today only a list's, runs for files on a view too, and the bridge from the element's events to <code>send()</code>.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:02.096Z"
      }
    },
    "c12": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "for <ui-uploader folder>`, the events should be `ui-folder-dropped` etc",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:12:04.610Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "folder: picks or takes dropped folders instead of files, each folder's files with their paths in it",
      "offset": 902,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:12:45.156Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:05.148Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded in. A <code>folder</code> uploader sends <code>ui-folder-dragged-over</code>, <code>ui-folder-dragged-out</code>, <code>ui-folder-dropping</code> and <code>ui-folder-dropped</code>, then <code>ui-file-dropped</code> for each file in the folder, with its path. Spanish:  <code>carpeta-soltada</code> and the rest.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:03.833Z"
      }
    },
    "c13": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "I think we can get the file handle in Safari/FF based on the File object\n\nThe handler should be able to get the file data, e.g. to process text inline.  `set contents to the file as text | json | binary` which would wait",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:13:33.850Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Safari and Firefox give none",
      "offset": 3276,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:14:26.693Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:06.568Z",
          "html": "<p>Partly right, and the plan said too little. What each browser gives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>File</code>, everywhere:  name, size, kind, last changed, and its contents, read as often as you like while the page is open.</li>\n<li>An entry, on a DROP, everywhere, Safari and Firefox too (<code>webkitGetAsEntry()</code>):  walks a dropped folder and gives each file's path.  It can't write the file or be kept past a reload.</li>\n<li>A handle (<code>getAsFileSystemHandle()</code>, the File System Access API), which can write and be kept for another day:  Chrome and Edge only.</li>\n<li>A folder picked in the dialog:  each file's path (<code>webkitRelativePath</code>) everywhere, no entry.</li>\n<li>No browser gets a handle from the <code>File</code> itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the events carry the file, its entry where there is one, and its handle in Chrome and Edge.</p>\n<p>Reading it:  your <code>set contents to the file as text</code> (<code>as json</code>, <code>as binary</code>), waiting for the read, is in this phase now, since Q7 settled on \"file\". As new Spell words they're in <a href=\"#q14\">Q14</a> for your OK.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:05.884Z"
      }
    },
    "c14": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "see above",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:14:50.254Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "No folder drag events",
      "offset": 3811,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:07.850Z",
          "html": "<p>Done with C12 and C13:  a browser can't tell a folder from a file while dragging, so a <code>folder</code> uploader names its drag events for what it takes, <code>ui-folder-dragged-over</code> / <code>-out</code>.  A plain file dropped on it is turned away, with its reason.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:07.804Z"
      }
    },
    "c15": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "in spell, this could be `drop-zone={view}`",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:15:32.959Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "drop-zone=\"editor\"",
      "offset": 4604,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:15:39.934Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:09.033Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded in:  in Spell, <code>drop-zone={view}</code> makes the view's own element the zone;  in JavaScript, the property set to an element. The view is also where the events start up Spell's chain (C9).</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:09.624Z"
      }
    },
    "c16": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "so I can do before/while/after ?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:16:41.896Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Same shape as the tree's ui-selecting",
      "offset": 5536,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:16:53.242Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:10.572Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, mapped onto Spell's own rule:  an <code>-ing</code> event can be cancelled, a past one can't.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before:  <code>ui-file-dropping</code> per file (Spell:  <code>can take a file if: ...</code>, a pile's drop rule);  <code>ui-uploading</code>, was <code>ui-upload</code> (Spell:  <code>when uploading:</code>, <code>cancel uploading</code>).</li>\n<li>While:  <code>ui-file-dragged-over</code> / <code>-out</code> (<code>while a file is dragged over it:</code>);  <code>ui-upload-progress</code> (<code>while uploading:</code>, put back when it ends).</li>\n<li>After:  <code>ui-file-dropped</code> (<code>when a file is dropped on it:</code>), <code>ui-uploaded</code>, <code>ui-upload-failed</code> (<code>when a file is uploaded:</code>, <code>when a file fails to upload:</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cancelling <code>ui-file-dropped</code> was in the plan before:  wrong by Spell's rule, now the <code>-ing</code> one. The new Spell words wait for your OK in <a href=\"#q14\">Q14</a>.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:11.272Z"
      }
    },
    "c17": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "but pretty!  and stylable. Should be a slot.\nShould have slots for icon, message, properties for uploading message, etc.",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:17:47.056Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Browse button joined to it, the way a form's file field looks",
      "offset": 7665,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:18:38.044Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:11.801Z",
          "html": "<p>Folded in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slots:  <code>icon</code>, <code>message</code> and <code>button</code>, each falling back to the uploader's own.</li>\n<li>Properties for the words it shows, translated by default:  <code>message</code>, <code>drop-message</code>, <code>uploading-message</code> (\"Uploading {done} of {count}\"), <code>done-message</code>.</li>\n<li>Pretty:  checked against the designer's rules, light and dark.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mockup has a new \"Its slots\" example and a page's own restyle through the tokens (<code>p9-uploader-slots-*.png</code>).</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:13.064Z"
      }
    },
    "c18": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "let's call apperance=\"field\" one `input` since ui-field means the thing surrounding an input.\n\nNote in your mockup that the \"Browse\" button didn't look very good with the input field -- borders didn't line up, etc",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:19:36.728Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "four mockups are one page",
      "offset": 8140,
      "edited": "2026-11-20T00:20:57.414Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:13.019Z",
          "html": "<p>Renamed:  the <code>inline</code> look is <code>input</code> now (Spanish <code>entrada</code>), its part <code>input</code> too. The mockup and its pictures follow (<code>p9-uploader-input-*.png</code>).</p>\n<p>The Browse button:  measured, it's the same height as the field and the edges meet, but it has no border and 8px corners against the field's 10px.  <code>&lt;ui-input&gt;</code>'s own docs draw its Search button the same way, so it's the input's look, not the mockup's.  Per the house style that's your call:  <a href=\"#q13\">Q13</a> asks whether to fix it in the input.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:14.938Z"
      }
    },
    "c19": {
      "anchor": "p-file-upload-in-spell#field-5",
      "kind": "field",
      "label": "File Upload In Spell",
      "excerpt": "You picked C, your own option: a new Spell UI element, <ui-uploader> , that take",
      "text": "`on-upload` ?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-20T00:21:47.682Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "<ui-input>'s action",
      "offset": 9988,
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-20T01:06:14.272Z",
          "html": "<p>A real fork, so it's the third choice set in the phase's Choices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A, recommended:  keep <code>action={...}</code>.  In Spell UI every <code>on-</code> attribute is a listener for the event of that name (<code>on-ui-uploading</code>), so that stays true.</li>\n<li>B:  <code>on-upload={...}</code>, as you asked;  the one <code>on-</code> that isn't a listener, with no <code>upload</code> event.</li>\n<li>C:  no attribute:  a handler of <code>ui-uploading</code> takes the upload, Spell's own \"a handler that runs takes it\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Built as A unless you pick otherwise.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-20T00:55:16.547Z"
      }
    }
  },
  "on": {}
